# chopshop-jev: the CALLER's configuration, for BOTH backends.
#
# Copy this wherever your services take their environment (a systemd
# EnvironmentFile, a compose `env_file`, or exported in the shell that runs the
# annotator). The LOCAL SERVICE has its own template -- see
# `chopshop-laya.env.example` beside this file; this one is the side that asks
# the questions.
#
# The backend is `JEV_ENDPOINT` alone and nothing else: both services speak the
# same wire protocol (`POST /v1/systemone`), so there is no adapter, no second
# response shape and no code path. Switching back is unsetting the variable.
# Neither block names a machine -- loopback, or the address your own box answers
# on. The annotator reads ONLY the JEV_*/TYPESAFE_*/LAYA_* keys named here out of
# a dotfile, so an unrelated secret sitting next to them is never picked up.

# --- backend A: hosted (TypeSafe System One) --------------------------------
# This is the default when JEV_ENDPOINT is unset. Needs an API key.
JEV_ENDPOINT=https://api.typesafe.ai/v1/systemone
JEV_API_KEY=<your hosted key>
# JEV_MODEL=jev-latest                # the hosted default; JEV_MODEL is optional
#
# The historical aliases still work, so an existing deployment keeps working:
# TYPESAFE_API_KEY / TYPESAFE_ENDPOINT / TYPESAFE_MODEL.

# --- backend B: local laya-serve (the alternate) ----------------------------
# Same wire protocol, measured: state never leaves the box, no per-call cost,
# ~2 s warm against ~40 s for the hosted call. The token is the SAME value
# deploy/chopshop-laya.env.example sets as LAYA_API_KEY -- either name works
# here, which is what makes this a cutover and not a rename.
# LAYA_API_KEY=<the shared secret>
# JEV_ENDPOINT=http://127.0.0.1:8797/v1/systemone
# JEV_MODEL=typed-decisions           # NAMED, not left to routing: English text
#                                     # routes to the `english` checkpoint on its
#                                     # own, and on a real manifest that answered
#                                     # `render_or_tool_failure` where
#                                     # `typed-decisions` answered the actual
#                                     # `palette_mismatch`.

# --- what either backend needs ---------------------------------------------
# JEV_HTTP_TIMEOUT=20        # the HTTP call's own budget (seconds), distinct
#                            # from the runner's 180 s subprocess budget.

# The floor is ADVISORY: it never changes an answer, only which ones are listed
# in `low_confidence`. 0.6 is the shipped default and it is NOT calibrated for
# every backend -- the recorded per-vocabulary, per-backend masses are in
# `scripts/jev_annotate.py` (OBSERVED_CONFIDENCE_RANGES) and summarised in the
# README, so pick the number from your own evidence. Out of 0..1 is refused.
# JEV_CONFIDENCE_FLOOR=0.6

# --- generation side (scripts/slop_cannon.py only) --------------------------
# No default, deliberately: an unset endpoint exits 2 naming the variable
# rather than aiming at somebody's box and reporting "unreachable".
# CHOPSHOP_FLUX_ENDPOINT=

# --- who reads what ---------------------------------------------------------
#   scripts/jev_annotate.py   JEV_*/TYPESAFE_*/LAYA_* from the environment,
#                             then ~/.hermes/.env, then the repo .env
#   chopshop-svg runner.py    JEV_ANNOTATOR, JEV_API_KEY or TYPESAFE_API_KEY or
#                             LAYA_API_KEY, JEV_ENDPOINT, JEV_MODEL
#   scripts/slop_cannon.py    --endpoint / --jev-endpoint, falling back to
#                             CHOPSHOP_FLUX_ENDPOINT / JEV_ENDPOINT; forwards
#                             JEV_API_KEY or LAYA_API_KEY down to the annotator
#   systemd / compose         EnvironmentFile / env_file -- the same keys
